{
  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "thm:unique-bijective-reduction",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Suppose \\(T_a:\\mathcal H\\to\\mathcal G_a\\) and \\(T_a^*:\\mathcal G_a\\to\\mathcal H\\) are bijective for \\(a=0,1\\). Then \\(\\mathcal N_a(P)=\\mathcal K_a(P)=\\{0\\}\\), each bridge fiber is a singleton, \\(\\mathcal C_P=\\{0\\}\\), and \\(\\Phi_P\\) is either empty or the singleton containing the unique bridge score; it is the latter whenever that score is square-integrable. Whenever the hypotheses and conclusion of \\(\\mathrm{thm:quotient-efficiency}\\) apply, this unique score is \\(\\phi_{\\mathrm{eff}}\\). Unconditionally, at a strictly positive finite-support law for which the square arm matrices are nonsingular, the full-rank joint-solvability germ is relatively open in the probability simplex, \\(\\mathcal T_P=L_0^2(P)\\), and the unique square-integrable bridge score is the canonical gradient.",
    "depends_on": [
      "def:score-family",
      "def:normal-space",
      "thm:quotient-efficiency"
    ],
    "justification": "The repaired theorem separates algebraic uniqueness, conditional general efficiency, and unconditional finite full-rank efficiency.",
    "gap": "Bijectivity does not itself create paths or guarantee an unbounded product score is square-integrable.",
    "consumer": "Finite full-rank unique-bridge software retains the familiar canonical score endpoint.",
    "proof_tex": "Bijectivity of \\(T_a\\) implies injectivity, hence \\(\\mathcal N_a(P)=\\ker T_a=\\{0\\}\\).  Bijectivity of \\(T_a^*\\) likewise gives \\(\\mathcal K_a(P)=\\{0\\}\\).  If two outcome bridges existed, their difference would lie in \\(\\mathcal N_a(P)\\), and if two treatment bridges existed, their difference would lie in \\(\\mathcal K_a(P)\\); thus both fibers are singletons.  Every generator in \\(\\mathcal C_P\\) contains either \\(u\\in\\mathcal N_a(P)\\) or \\(m\\in\\mathcal K_a(P)\\), so it is zero and \\(\\mathcal C_P=\\{0\\}\\).  The score family consequently has at most one member, and it has exactly one when the unique displayed score is in \\(L_0^2(P)\\).  Under \\(\\mathrm{thm:quotient-efficiency}\\), projection onto the zero space leaves that score unchanged.\n\nNow suppose the observed sample space is finite, all cells have positive probability, and each square arm matrix is nonsingular.  Invertibility is open because the determinant is continuous and nonzero.  On a sufficiently small positive simplex neighborhood, \\(h_a=B_a^{-1}b_a\\) and \\(q_a=B_a^{-\\top}d\\) therefore exist uniquely and depend smoothly on the cell probabilities; thus the joint-solvability/full-rank germ is relatively open.  For every bounded mean-zero score \\(s\\),\n\\[\np_t(o)=p(o)\\{1+ts(o)\\}\n\\]\nis positive and belongs to this germ for small \\(|t|\\), with score \\(s\\).  Bounded mean-zero functions are dense in \\(L_0^2(P)\\), so \\(\\mathcal T_P=L_0^2(P)\\).  Differentiating the two finite matrix equations as in the outcome-comparator proof gives the unique bridge score as a gradient; because the tangent is all of \\(L_0^2(P)\\), it is the canonical gradient without any separate general lifting assumption.",
